WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
A measurement is not a perfect label attached to an object.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.680
It is our best estimate, made with a particular instrument and method.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.560
When you read a ruler, the final digit is an estimate between markings.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.020
That is why a careful measurement should communicate both a value and its uncertainty.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.640
Precision describes how finely we can repeat or resolve a measurement.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.300
Accuracy describes how close the result is to the accepted value.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.960
A result can be very precise and still be inaccurate if the instrument has a zero error.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.080
Significant figures tell the reader how much information a measured number carries.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:46.040
In zero point zero zero four five zero, the leading zeros only locate the decimal place, so they do not count.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.300
The four, five, and final zero do count.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.860
That gives three significant figures.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.260
In contrast, zeros between non-zero digits are significant.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.000
Write numbers in scientific notation when a string of zeros could confuse the intended precision.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.208
The coefficient makes the significant figures visible.

00:01:05.208 --> 00:01:12.968
To round two point seven four six to three significant figures, keep two, seven, and four, then inspect the next digit, six.

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:16.308
Because it is five or greater, increase the last kept digit.

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.668
The result is two point seven five.

00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:25.008
In a multi-step calculation, keep a guard digit during the working and round only the final answer.

00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:28.848
Rounding at every line can push the result away from the measured information.

00:01:28.848 --> 00:01:32.664
The final format should match the precision justified by the data.

00:01:32.664 --> 00:01:39.864
For addition and subtraction, the rule is about decimal places, not the total number of significant figures.

00:01:39.864 --> 00:01:42.904
Add twelve point one one and zero point three.

00:01:42.904 --> 00:01:49.004
The second measurement is known only to the tenths place, so the answer must stop at the tenths place.

00:01:49.004 --> 00:01:54.004
The unrounded sum is twelve point four one, which becomes twelve point four.

00:01:54.004 --> 00:01:58.304
The hundredths digit cannot be justified by the less precise measurement.

00:01:58.304 --> 00:02:01.632
Always identify the operation before choosing the rounding rule.

00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:05.392
For multiplication and division, count significant figures.

00:02:05.392 --> 00:02:07.912
Two point five has two significant figures.

00:02:07.912 --> 00:02:09.932
Three point four two has three.

00:02:09.932 --> 00:02:18.692
Their product is eight point five five, but the final result must have two significant figures because the least precise input has two.

00:02:18.692 --> 00:02:21.552
Rounded correctly, that is eight point six.

00:02:21.552 --> 00:02:24.792
Do not confuse this with the decimal-place rule for addition.

00:02:24.792 --> 00:02:30.096
These two rules are frequent multiple-choice traps, so write the operation at the top of your solution.

00:02:30.096 --> 00:02:35.733
Suppose a length is measured as twenty point zero plus or minus zero point one centimetres.

00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:39.676
Zero point one centimetres is the absolute uncertainty.

00:02:39.676 --> 00:02:43.736
Divide it by twenty point zero centimetres and the units cancel.

00:02:43.736 --> 00:02:49.296
The relative uncertainty is zero point zero zero five, or zero point five percent.

00:02:49.296 --> 00:02:57.016
A small absolute uncertainty is not enough to judge a result; its size must be compared with the measurement itself.

00:02:57.016 --> 00:03:00.048
Relative and percentage uncertainty make that comparison possible.

00:03:00.048 --> 00:03:04.288
For a sum or difference, add the absolute uncertainties.

00:03:04.288 --> 00:03:13.688
If x has uncertainty delta x and y has uncertainty delta y, then the uncertainty in x plus or minus y is approximately their sum.

00:03:13.688 --> 00:03:17.768
For a product or quotient, add the relative uncertainties instead.

00:03:17.768 --> 00:03:22.128
These are standard maximum-uncertainty rules used at this level.

00:03:22.128 --> 00:03:27.008
Statistical uncertainty can be treated differently when repeated measurements and random errors are analyzed.

00:03:27.008 --> 00:03:29.952
The question wording tells you which model to use.

00:03:29.952 --> 00:03:33.872
Error and uncertainty are related, but they are not identical.

00:03:33.872 --> 00:03:40.692
If the accepted length is ten centimetres and your measured value is ten point two centimetres, the percentage error compares their

00:03:40.692 --> 00:03:43.192
difference with the accepted value.

00:03:43.192 --> 00:03:48.792
Uncertainty, on the other hand, describes the range or limitation attached to your measurement.

00:03:48.792 --> 00:03:53.412
Random errors change from trial to trial and may be reduced by averaging.

00:03:53.412 --> 00:03:58.464
Systematic errors shift results in one direction and require calibration or correction.

00:03:58.464 --> 00:04:03.524
Before submitting a measurement problem, use four checks.

00:04:03.524 --> 00:04:06.084
First, are the units compatible?

00:04:06.084 --> 00:04:09.864
Second, did you count significant figures correctly?

00:04:09.864 --> 00:04:16.704
Third, did you use the decimal-place rule for addition and the significant-figure rule for multiplication?

00:04:16.704 --> 00:04:21.704
Fourth, is the uncertainty or error reported in the requested form?

00:04:21.704 --> 00:04:25.974
Keep extra digits during the working, then round the final answer once.

00:04:25.974 --> 00:04:32.232
This routine turns a confusing-looking calculation into a sequence of small decisions.
